				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody"><span style="font-style: italic">Another roleplay story brought to you by the "Guild Scribe" Sriani Heartofwolf. Enjoy and post/PM comments at will. Disclaimer: Poetic license has been invoked, events have been changed to fit the story. </span>
<br />

<br />
Deep in the frozen wasteland of the Great Divide, beyond the ice-encrusted hills, the deep tunnels of the dwarves, and the steady rumble of Frost Giants crossing the land, a thin whisper of smoke rose high into the air, untouched by any wind. It came from a small fire, coaxed into life with only the tiniest supply of fuel. Beside it crouched a young woman, huddled in layers of clothing and furs, she was almost impossible to see. She mixed a dry trail mix in a cup with melted snow, creating a thick porridge. As the sun rose over the snow covered land, she began to eat in the all encompassing silence.
<br />

<br />
At the sound of footsteps crunching through the snow, she looked up, her hand immedietly on the hilt of her sword strapped to her back. Over the hill trudged a figure, supporting itself with a long staff. She watched it come, wary, but she did not sense any danger from the creature. At last it approached her fire, and she looked into the eyes of an ancient high elf. 
<br />

<br />
"Sriani."  It croaked, and smiled warmly. The woman blinked in confusion.
<br />

<br />
"How do you know my name?" She asked. The old elf smiled again.
<br />

<br />
"I know many things. I sought you, always a step behind in your journeys. Through the deserts of Ro, and the mountains of Highpass, deep into the ruins of Old Paineel." He leaned on his staff and watched her. Sriani shoook her head.
<br />

<br />
"Why are you following me? Who are you?" 
<br />

<br />
"I follow you and others of your kind. There is a message I must deliver."
<br />

<br />
"My kind? Humans?"
<br />

<br />
"No..mortals." He smiled again, and the sun rose over the far hills at that moment, seeming to crown the old man in a blaze of gold. Sriani stepped back and fell to one knee.
<br />

<br />
"You are..."
<br />

<br />
"Yes. I am SOE, the God of All Norrath, and I must deliver a message to you. The gates between this world and the other worlds of Zek have weakened and will soon break. Within weeks, all worlds of Zek will become one. It is time for you to return to your Covenant."
<br />

<br />
Sriani gasped, and pulled her cloak around her, as if stricken with a sudden chill. 
<br />

<br />
"I have been gone for months. I don't know if I am still welcome." 
<br />

<br />
"Trust in me, your welcome never faded. When the worlds of Zek merge, your Covenant must be together, whole, to weather the chaos that will come." He reached out and laid a warm leathery hand on her head, gently stroking her hair, like a father. "Be at ease child, your loneliness is over. Go now, today, and hesitate for no reason. There is very little time." 
<br />

<br />
With that, he turned into the sun and seemed to wink out of existence. She could never be sure why, when she looked back on that day, but she trusted him, something in her knew he spoke truth. Immedietly she packed her belongings, and set off over the frozen hills towards the Butcherblock Mountains, and home.
<br />

<br />
***
<br />

<br />
At midday, the sun was high over the treetops, warming the stones of the fortress. Entwood the Druid sat atop the wall by the gates, looking out over the land, his eye keen for movement. Below him in the courtyard, hundreds of people moved back and forth in a constant bustle, leading horses, carrying bags, pulling carts. Weapons shone brilliantly in the sun, armor gleamed as if made of glass. The different languages of all the folk of Norrath mixed to create a dull roar of sound as the guild carried about its daily business. 
<br />

<br />
Entwood was just about to unpack his lunch when he spotted a lone figure make its way down the forest path towards the guild hall. He stood, summoning the powers within him to borrow the sight of an eagle flying high above, directing it to gaze apon this lone traveler. What he saw made him smile.  Immedietly he ran down the steps into the courtyard, dashing accross with barely a word for the people he jostled, into the main hall.
<br />

<br />
At the long wooden table, a few folk sat down to their midday meal, and at the head of the table sat the guild's fiery beautiful leader, Raveena. As always she was surrounded in papers and books, but even so an expression of calm assurance was on her face. She looked up and smiled as Entwood bowed to her. 
<br />

<br />
"Hail Entwood. What news from the walls?"  
<br />

<br />
"Raveena, Sriani has returned." His grin looked fit to split his face. 
<br />

<br />
Raveena seemed to still a moment, thinking back to the tall red-haired warrior who had been such an integral part of the pack and then suddenly gone away. Scouts had reported she travelled the land alone, slaying monsters and demons, perhaps some in her own heart as well. After all this time, Raveena wondered why the woman returned. 
<br />

<br />
"Fetch her at the gates Entwood and bring her to me."  
<br />

<br />
"M'lady." Entwood bowed again and rushed off. Within moments he returned, and behind him the road-dusted Sriani. She and Raveena stared at each other a moment, and then Sriani knelt before her and bowed her head.
<br />

<br />
"Raveena, my Guild Mistress. Please accept my apologies for my long absence, I beg to deliver a message and be returned to my place in the Pack."
<br />

<br />
Raveena waved a hand as if to swat away a fly.
<br />

<br />
"Your place was never taken. What message?"  
<br />

<br />
Sriani looked up, her eyes troubled yet full of wonder.
<br />

<br />
"In the Great Divide, a man came to my camp. An ancient high elf on a staff, crowned with the sun. It was SOE."
<br />

<br />
Raveena gasped.
<br />

<br />
"SOE? The God of All Norrath who causes unexpected changes and irrational shifts of reality?" 
<br />

<br />
"It was him. He spoke to me." And Sriani then told the message he had given her. After she finished speaking, there was stunned silence in the hall. 
<br />

<br />
"So. The worlds will merge into one." Raveena mused. "Other guilds and peoples, all evil and good will come crashing together. Thousands who once lived separate lives will now occupy one space. It will be chaos."
<br />

<br />
By this time the hall had filled with all the members of the guild, as the tale of Sriani's message had spread quickly throughout the compound.  Their murmers of concern rose into the rafters.
<br />

<br />
"It will sweep over us like a wave, it will destroy all we have." One voice called out in panic. 
<br />

<br />
With a snarl, Samackon slammed his fist down to the table, shaking the cups and plates, causing a few to draw back in surprise. He glared up at them, meeting everyone's eyes in turn. Silence now hung heavy about them. "Damn you, we will not be destroyed. We will fight, and stand strong as we've always done."
<br />

<br />
A few nodded, there were whispers of agreement. At his side, Callidan nodded gravely. Raveena sighed, rubbing her temples. Her face was troubled.
<br />

<br />
"Please, everyone, do not panic. Go back to your duties and routines, the God SOE said we still have three weeks before the gates crumble. Officers, stay here, we must discuss our options." 
<br />

<br />
The word of the Guild Mistress was too powerful to be disobeyed. The guild slowly filtered out of the hall, moving with stunned slowness, whispering amongst themselves. The news was completely unexpected. Never, in all the histories of Norrath had the gates between worlds collapsed. There were tales of folk possessed of the power to move between the worlds, giving sacrifice to SOE for the privilege, but never entire worlds moving into one. 
<br />

<br />
***
<br />

<br />
For the next few weeks, Raveena and her Council of Officers remained cloistered in the Mail Hall, deliberating on the news. Scouts had been sent early on to verify the news, and they all brought back the same tale. All the guilds of Rallos Zek had been given the message by SOE, even those who walked alone, the solo people, as they were known, knew of the impending chaos. Everyone was in a state of wary readiness, not knowing what to expect, but prepared for the worst. Three scouts hand-picked by the mages and wizards were sent between the worlds to gather intelligence about the guilds of Sullon and Tallon. 
<br />

<br />
They returned, all three, with the same heart-heavy news. The people of Sullon and Tallon were rich with spoils of war, experienced in the art of death, and bloodthirsty for more. They awaited the eventual collapse of the gates with almost cheerful anticipation. Some, not yet bowed under the yolk of evil, were interested in forming pre-merge alliances to solidify a foothold in the new world. These guilds of like mind to Covenant had sent gifts and offers with the scouts to Raveena. 
<br />

<br />
At last, mere days before the collapse of the gates was said to occur, she announced their new alliance, the Awakening. A banding together of guilds from all three worlds, their combined strength enough to hold back the shattering might of evil that called itself, on one world, simply <span style="font-style: italic">Hate</span>.
<br />

<br />
***
<br />

<br />
As the sun rose over the mountains of Butcherblock, Covenant of the Wolf stood together, weapons in hand. There was fear in some, pride in all. They smiled encouragingly to each other and awaited the fateful moment.
<br />

<br />
When the gates collapsed, there was a great crack, a sound so great and terrible it sent some folk to their knees. This was followed by a tremor in the earth, so powerful that trees fell and stones tumbled from the mountains.  And from all sides came the booming cry of a thousand voices raised in challenge. They had come, and they came for blood.
<br />

<br />
Most of that day was spent in endless battle, such a battle that each moment blended into another until the entire world was an impossible blur of war. All over Norrath, the people of the three worlds clashed, no one knew who was friend and who was foe, for that day all were suspect. 
<br />

<br />
It continued for days, and as it rose the spirits of many fell. The new world was unlike their old in all ways, there was death at every turn, traitors in every place, no where was safe from these terrible and cruel new folk. It came to a point that the guild could not even attempt a normal routine, and on the fateful night their Lord Ugloth fell to a lone man's sly tricks, they knew something needed to change.
<br />

<br />
**
<br />

