				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody"><span style="font-weight: bold">A Journey Begins</span>
<br />
<span style="font-style: italic">Disclaimer: Not being present for the first few days of the merger, or the raid on RZ that was ruined, I am forced to "make up" a few things here.  Also some of you may or may not agree with your character's parts but I like to use everyone in my tales. Thanks for your understanding I hope you enjoy the story.</span>
<br />

<br />
"We've been holed up in here for days. I'm sick of it."  Wickedness growled one evening, relaxing against the wall of the barracks. He was sharpening his weapon repeatedly on a stone, so that the edge was by now keen enough to split a hair. 
<br />

<br />
"It's too dangerous to venture beyond the walls. They've swarmed the area."  Greenelk sighed and tossed the biscuit he'd been munching on to the ground. "Inaction is eating at me."
<br />

<br />
For days, the guild had been trapped in their own fortress, unable to break through the legions of enemies that surrounded them, or even to get messages to friendly folk. It seemed the entire world was cut off from them.  As their ill ease grew, tempers rose and flared. Some members had even been caught fighting out in the courtyard, a rare sight here. 
<br />

<br />
Raveena wasn't blind to her guild's unhappiness, nor was she ignorant of what was at stake.  Their futures as wolves, the noble legend they had built over generations fighting on Rallos Zek for the title of Time Flagged. Through it all, through the death, the traitorous backstabbing, the drama, and the good times, the victories, the love of family - she had steered at the helm of this great ship. It had never been easy, the Gods knew it felt thankless at times, but with a steady hand she'd led them into the Elements. And now she was faced with her greatest decision ever. 
<br />

<br />
"It can be done." At the Council table, Samackon looked up from a stack of books, dusty old tomes of lore from the Wizard Guild. "I almost didn't find it, buried under all this useless verbage, but the legend reads that when the worlds merge into one, SOE will grant the gates to part into the other worlds for those who wish to travel permanently to them." 
<br />

<br />
He was pointing at a paragraph in one of the books.  Beside him, Zanthos, another wizard, nodded sagely.
<br />

<br />
"We can do it, if we combine our powers with that of the mages. Together we should be able to use SOE's 'grace period' to open a gate into another world."
<br />

<br />
"And escape this chaos." Raveena mused from the head of the table. She was almost lost in shadow and hidden behind stacks of papers. The Council had been meeting for many days, so long that at night most just lay on the floor for a few hours sleep, only to wake and begin their talks and research anew. They had let the torches burn down almost to nothing, lost in their deliberations.  "A new world free of these killers, where we can advance freely."
<br />

<br />
A few seats down from her, Callidan sighed loudly. 
<br />

<br />
"I think we need to stay and fight. To leave now would be backing down." 
<br />

<br />
"Backing down from what exactly? We have honor and pride enough to know when a change is needed. If we want to reach Time, we need to do it where we will be unharrassed by low-lifes such as have infested Zek." Adaniel met the wizard's eyes across the table. "On the other worlds, SOE's magic prevents people from attacking each other. All the powers of every man and woman are focused on achieving a single goal. That is what we want, and need."
<br />

<br />
Ugloth nodded to his fellow warrior and officer.
<br />

<br />
"Adaniel is correct, and well said my friend. I think the best thing for the guild is to leave this land and find one where you can reach your goals in peace. I can already see the decay apon this land, eating away at its heart which was once so fierce, it is now nothing but a diseased thing ready to die. No good will come of remaining here, only to die with it."
<br />

<br />
Raveena glanced up with a dangerous gleam in her eye.
<br />

<br />
"What do you mean 'your goals' Ugloth? Are they not your goals as well?"
<br />

<br />
He looked away from her, unable to meet her fiery gaze. 
<br />

<br />
"I will not go with you this time m'lady." 
<br />

<br />
Raveena glanced at Trefellgion. He shrugged, a confused look on his face. She turned back to the barbarian warrior.
<br />

<br />
"You are staying here? You just said this was a diseased land ready to die."
<br />

<br />
"Yes, I know. But something in me knows I am not going to be here much longer either. I would rather see out my last days in the land I love, even if it is a sick thing, than attempt to move now." Ugloth was looking out the window, watching the clouds drift across the blue sky. He did not want anyone to see the pain in his eyes. 
<br />

<br />
Raveena would have said more, but she could read her old friend well, and knew he wanted to leave the matter at that. With a quiet sigh, and a quick sniff to pull back any tears, she turned back to the Council. 
<br />

<br />
"Well then it is decided. We will take this chance and leave Zek before it destroys itself. I want scouts gone within the hour, Samackon, organize their travel to the other worlds. I want to know who is there, where they are in progression, their alliances, everything you can."
<br />

<br />
Samackon nodded and hurried out, a book tucked under one arm. 
<br />

<br />
"I will tell the guild. The rest of you, prepare your classes for the move. I want to be out of here within the week." 
<br />

<br />
There were nods of assent and murmurs of "M'lady" as the officers and class leaders rushed out of the hall to begin the task of moving the guild. Raveena stood, stretched the kinks out of her back, and turned to Trefellgion.
<br />

<br />
"Will you gather them in the courtyard?" She asked softly. He nodded, then reached over to hug her swiftly, tightly, and then he was gone.
<br />

<br />
She looked about the empty room and at last allowed herself to shed the tears that had been held back for so long.
<br />

<br />
***
<br />

<br />
Under the midday sun, the guild crowded the courtyard. The air was heavy with excitement and a tense fear. Their talk died down as Raveena stepped out of the Main Hall. She stayed on the steps, so that she was a bit above the crowd. For a few moments she simply looked over them all, taking in their faces, reminding herself why she was making this decision.
<br />

<br />
<span style="font-style: italic">Rav this speech was so good I had to use it, and really didn't need to edit it all that much.</span>
<br />

<br />
"We have always prided ourselves on being the nice people on Rallos. We were the guild that went out of our way to help out other guilds. One time we sent 2 groups of Covenant away from our raid to help a new guild called Mercs of Norrath kill Ragefire after their entire guild wiped on it. We killed Ragefire for them, revived their guild, rebuffed them and then left and went back to our own raid in Ssrashaza Temple.
<br />

<br />
We were the ones that helped out new guilds get corpses after they wiped on a raid and were unable to return to them when griefers held them back.
<br />

<br />
We were the ones that found Ixiblatt up in Burning Woods, with no other guilds in the zone and we started checking around the newbie guilds to find out which cleric did the turn in and failed on their epic mob. We found that cleric (Fragilecat) and then killed ragefire for her so she could continue her quest.
<br />

<br />
We didn't know any of these people, we had no close ties, we weren't allied, we didn't raid with them and we didn't expect anything in return. We were just the nice people on Rallos.
<br />

<br />
But Rallos is gone. And Zek is no substitute.
<br />

<br />
Because of this, the Council and I have decided to take advantage of a weakening in the gates between this world and the others of Norrath, and leave Zek. We will go to a world where we can progress and achieve our goals in peace. 
<br />

<br />
This was no easy decision for us, you know we've been in deliberations for days. You are free to choose to come with us or stay here, no one is forced to do what they do not wish to.  Anyone staying I will help you find a good home in another guild before we leave. But our move will be swift. I do not wish to hold up our progression any further by staying, so I beg you all to make your decisions now."
<br />

<br />
She stopped, wanting to say more, but there were no words for all the things she felt. They were all staring up at her in differing states of shock. As the silence deepened, she heard someone slowly start to clap. Then another, then another. Within seconds, the fortress boomed with cheers and clapping, loud enough that the birds flew out of the trees in surprise. 
<br />

<br />
"Raveena!! Three cheers for Raveena!" Someone called out, she suspected Trocair, it sounded like the cleric. Whoever it was, the call was taken up immedietly as they cheered her name, roaring it up at the sky, and quickly it faded from a roar to a great howl, such as had never been heard before. In the woods and mountains, all across the land, people paused a moment as the mighty wolf howl crossed over them. 
<br />

<br />
"This is where our journey begins." Trefellgion smiled, suddenly beside her. She smiled at him.
<br />

<br />
"I wonder where it shall take us this time." She mused, as they went back into the hall together. 
<br />

<br />
****
<br />

<br />
<span style="font-style: italic"> Well, that's where we stand now, waiting to go! So for now I shall pause this tale, I am sure more will be forthcoming in the days ahead as we move to MR!</span><div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br /><a href="http://www.magelo.com/eq_view_profile.html?num=956867" target="_blank" class="postlink">~Sriani HeartofWolf~</a>
<br />
